 To convert a given phrase to its Morse Code representation using International Morse Code standard in MATLAB, you can create a lookup table for the Morse Code representations of characters, convert the input phrase to uppercase, and then apply element-wise indexing to get the corresponding Morse Code sequences. Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates this approach:
```matlab
% Define Morse code lookup table
morseCodeTable = struct('char', cellstr(double(81:97)'-64), 'morseCode', {''. ' .-', '-...' , '-.-.', '-..', '.', '..-.' , '--.' , '---', '.--.', '--', '---...', '--.-.', '.-..', '--.', '-----', '.----', '..---', '...--', '....-', '.....', '-....', '--...', '---..', '----.'});
% Input phrase
inputPhrase = 'HELLO WORLD';
% Convert input phrase to uppercase
inputPhraseUpper = upper(inputPhrase);
% Get Morse code representation of each character in the input phrase
morseCodes = cellfun(@(x) morseCodeTable.morseCode{find(strcmp(char(inputPhraseUpper), x) == 1)}, char(inputPhraseUpper));
% Combine Morse codes into a single string with inter-character spacing
outputString = strjoin(morseCodes, ' ');
% Display Morse code representation of the input phrase
disp(outputString)
```
This code defines a lookup table `morseCodeTable` using MATLAB's `struct` function, which maps each character to its corresponding Morse code sequence. It then converts the input phrase to uppercase, uses `cellfun` to apply indexing to the lookup table based on each character in the input phrase, combines the resulting Morse code sequences into a single string with inter-character spacing, and finally displays the output.

